eft). To complete chow W1,W2,W3, need W1 and W3 (unseen: 3 and 2). \n- F2: to complete pung, need two more F2 (unseen: 3 left). \n- J3: to complete pung, need two more J3 (unseen: 3 left).\nProbabilities similar. But F2 is prevailing wind, so if I pung F2, I get Prevalent Wind (2) and Seat Wind (2) both? Actually Seat Wind is also F2, so one pung gives both patterns (4 points total). That's valuable. J3 pung gives Dragon Pung (2). W2 pung gives nothing special except maybe All Simples? But W2 is simple, so if I have no honors or terminals, All Simples (2) might apply, but I have F2 and J3, so not All Simples.\nSo F2 pung is most scoring. However, I only have one F2, need two more. Unseen 3, so possible.\nJ3 pung also gives 2 points.\nW2 pung gives 0 extra.\nThus, I should keep F2 and J3, discard W2. But W2 might be dangerous? Previous player did Chi W2, so they have a W2 in meld, maybe they don't need another, but others might.\nAlternatively, I could discard J3 or F2 to keep W2, but W2 offers less points.\nAlso, I have a pair of W9, which are terminals, so if I win, I get Pung of Terminals or Honors? No, pair doesn't count. But if I draw a third W9, I get a terminal pung (2 points). Unseen W9: 3 left.\nSo my winning tiles are: any third tile for W2, F2, J3, or W9. Also, if I draw W1 and W3, I can chow W2. But that requires two draws, not immediate.\nGiven I am in tenpai (ready) if I consider the concealed chow, I actually need to decide which single to keep. I have three singles: W2, F2, J3. I can only keep two because I have 5 leftover tiles but one must be the pair? Wait, my pair is W9, so the three singles are separate. So I have three singles and a pair. That's 5 tiles: W2, F2, J3, W9, W9. That's correct. So I need to discard one of the singles to get to 4 tiles (pair + two singles). But in Mahjong, when you have a concealed chow, you don't have to declare it; you can keep the tiles separate. But here, since T7,T8,T9 form a chow, I can mentally group them, leaving me with needing one meld and a pair. The pair is W9. The meld can be formed from one of the singles plus two more tiles. So I have three candidates for the meld. So I should keep all three singles? But that would be 6 tiles: W2, F2, J3, W9, W9. That's 6, but my hand only has 8 tiles total, and T7,T8,T9 are 3, so 8-3=5. So I have 5 tiles after grouping the chow: W2, F2, J3, W9, W9. That's 5. To be in tenpai, I need to discard one tile to have 4 tiles that are one away from completion. Currently, with 5 tiles, I am one tile away from having a complete hand if I draw a tile that makes a meld with one of the singles, but I have three singles. That's a multi-wait. Actually, I am in tenpai already? Let's check: If I draw a third W2, I have pung W2, and pair W9, and three chows -> win. If I draw third F2, win. If I draw third J3, win. If I draw third W9, I have pung W9, then I need a meld from W2,F2,J3. But then I would have two pairs? Actually, if I draw W9, I have three W9, which is a pung, so then my hand: three chows, pung W9, and leftover W2, F2, J3. That's two singles, no pair. So I would need a pair from W2,F2,J3. So drawing W9 does not put me in tenpai; it puts me needing a pair. So my current waits are for W2, F2, J3 to complete pung. Also, if I draw W1 and W3, but that's two draws.\nSo I am in tenpai for W2, F2, J3. That's three waits. Good.\nNow, which single is least useful? Discarding any of W2, F2, J3 would reduce my waits. If I discard W2, I lose wait for W2, but keep F2 and J3 waits. If I discard F2, lose F2 wait, etc. So I should discard the one with fewest unseen tiles or most dangerous.\nUnseen: W2: 2 left, F2: 3 left, J3: 3 left. So W2 has fewest, so maybe discard W2? But W2 is simple, less likely to give points. F2 gives 4 points if pung, J3 gives 2. So I want to keep F2 and J3.\nAlso, safety: Previous player did Chi W2, so they might not need W2, but others? Next player's discards: W5, B2, T8, F3, B9, T5, T3, W8, W7, F2. They discarded F2 once, so maybe they don't need F2. But F2 is wind, so could be risky. Across player: discarded F3, J3. So J3 might be safer? They discarded J3, so maybe they don't need J3.\nGiven that, I think discarding W2 is reasonable. It reduces my waits but keeps higher-value waits.\nBut wait, if I discard W2, my hand becomes: chows: W6-W8, T1-T3, T7-T9; pair: W9; singles: F2, J3. I am waiting for F2 or J3. That's two waits. Good.\nAlternatively, discarding J3 keeps waits for W2 and F2. But W2 has only 2 unseen, F2 has 3. So total 5 unseen tiles. Discarding W2 keeps waits for F2 (3) and J3 (3) = 6 unseen. So discarding W2 gives more waiting tiles.\nAlso, discarding W2 might be safer based on discards: next player discarded W7, W8, but not W2. Previous player used W2 in chow, so might not need. Across player discarded W2 once. So W2 is somewhat safe.\nThus, I choose to play W2.\n\nAnswer: Play W2"}
